---
// src/pages/_site-updates/2026-11-16-founders-tour-one-thing.astro
export const updateMeta = {
  date: '2026-11-16',
  title: 'Founders tour card asks for the one thing that would help most',
  tags: ['waitlist', 'lead-capture', 'founders', 'tour'],
};
---
<article class="border-l-4 border-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] pl-6">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">November 16, 2026</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
    The Your Tour Is Booked card on
    <a href="/pricing" class="text-[hsl(24,95%,53%)] hover:underline">/pricing</a> now has an
    optional question: what is the one thing that would make the biggest difference to the
    applicant's program.
  </p>

  <div class="mb-4">
    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Why</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
      The tour is built around what the applicant needs most. Asking right after the booking,
      while they are engaged, gives the founders that answer before the call instead of during it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">How it works</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
      The card shows a text box with the helper line "We'll build your tour around it" and a Send
      button. The answer is cleaned and checked for length in code points, the way the platform
      counts it, then posted to a new server route on the site, <code>/api/tour-focus</code>. That
      route holds the shared secret, takes only JSON from this site's own pages, brakes a flood
      from one address, reads at most 16 KB of request body, never logs the answer, and relays it
      to the platform with the application's submission key. A save shows "Thanks,
      we'll be ready." Any failure, including an API that does not have the endpoint yet, shows a
      quiet line saying the tour is still booked, and never changes the booking or the Add to
      Calendar buttons.
    </p>
  </div>
</article>
